Actually, let me reconsider the `latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs` function. It reads `size` bytes into `buffer`. In `latm_dmx_process`:
```c
u8 latm_buffer[4096];
u32 latm_frame_size = 4096;
if (!latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs(ctx->bs,&ctx->acfg, &latm_frame_size, latm_buffer, NULL)) break;
```

Wait, `latm_frame_size` is initialized to 4096. In `latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs`:
```c
size = 0;
while (1) {
    u32 tmp = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 8);
    size += tmp;
    if (tmp!=255) break;
}
if (gf_bs_available(bs) < size) {
    gf_bs_seek(bs, pos-3);
    return GF_FALSE;
}
if (nb_bytes) {
    *nb_bytes = (u32) size;
}
if (buffer) {
    gf_bs_read_data(bs, (char *) buffer, size);
}
```

So `size` is read from the input. If `size > 4096`, then `gf_bs_read_data(bs, latm_buffer, size)` writes beyond the 4096-byte stack buffer. That's a stack overflow. But the description says heap overflow.

Hmm. But wait - `gf_bs_available(bs) < size` check. If `size` is large but `gf_bs_available` is also large (because the input buffer is large), then it proceeds. The `latm_buffer` is only 4096 bytes on the stack. So if `size > 4096`, stack overflow.

But the description says heap-buffer-overflow READ of 9 bytes. So this isn't it.

